WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.080
A type of saturated fat
concentrated in meat and dairy

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.760
seems to cause cancer
to metastasize.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.000
Check out the video
to find out more.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:17.065
"What Causes Cancer to Metastasize
and How to Potentially Help Control it with Diet"

00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:22.680
Metastasis formation is the leading
cause of death in cancer patients.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.160
That’s how most people die of cancer.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.960
It’s not the primary tumor, but the
cancer spreading through the body.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.880
“[It’s] estimated that metastasis
is responsible for [90 percent]

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.520
of cancer deaths”, with little
progress made in stopping the spread

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.680
despite our modern
medical armamentarium.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.400
In fact, we can sometimes make it
worse: Therapy-Induced Metastasis.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.640
All the typical cancer treatments,
radiation, chemotherapy, surgery—

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.800
even just poking the tumors
with fine needle biopsies—

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.920
have the potential to
contribute to the problem.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.000
I mean, you can imagine
how cutting around a tumor,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.360
severing the blood vessels,
might lead to the

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.520
migration of residual tumor cells.
But why chemotherapy?

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.400
How might chemo
exacerbate metastases?

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.960
Despite reducing the size of
primary tumors, chemotherapy

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.360
can change the surrounding tissues,
resulting in an increased

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.360
escape of cancer cells
into the blood stream.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.040
Sometimes chemo/surgery/
radiation are entirely justified,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.280
but sometimes these treatments
can make things worse.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.440
If only we had a way to treat the
cause of the cancer spreading.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.840
The development of antimetastatic
therapies has been hampered

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.920
by the fact that we haven’t
been able to identity

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.920
the cells that initiate metastasis.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.400
But then, this landmark
study was published.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.560
Researchers found a subpopulation
of human cancer cells

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.800
“unique in their ability
to initiate metastasis”,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.920
all expressing high levels of
a fat receptor known as CD36,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.160
dubbed “the fat controller.”

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.360
It turns out palmitic acid or
a high-fat diet specifically

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.120
boosts the metastatic
potential of these cancer cells.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.160
Where do you find palmitic acid?

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.640
Although originally
discovered in palm oil,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.120
it’s most concentrated
in meat and dairy.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.320
“Emerging evidence shows
that palmitic acid . . .

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.080
serves as a signaling molecule
regulating the progression

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.840
and development of many
diseases at the molecular level”,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.120
and that’s the saturated fat
that is recognized by the

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.640
CD36 receptor on the cancer cells.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.520
And we know that’s to blame, because
if you block the CD36 receptor,

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.560
you block the metastases.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.920
Now this was for a human cancer,
however it was a human cancer

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.520
implanted into mice, but clinically
(meaning in cancer patients)

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.000
the presence of these CD36-studded
metastasis-initiating cells

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.480
does indeed correlate
with a poor prognosis.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.640
For example, CD36 appears to drive
the progression of brain tumors.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.040
If you look at the survival curves,
those with tumors with less

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.200
CD36 expression lived
significantly longer.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.120
The same with breast cancer mortality.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:19.960
No surprise, since “CD36 [appears
to play] a critical role in

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.680
[the] proliferation, migration, and…
growth of…breast cancer cells”.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.240
Inhibit CD36, and you can inhibit

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.760
“the migration and invasion of
the breast cancer cells.”

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:38.680
Cancer cell migration and invasion
before and after CD36 inhibition.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:43.600
And not just in “human melanoma-
and breast cancer-derived tumors.”

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.440
Now we suspect that “…CD36…drives
ovarian cancer progression

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.480
and metastasis” too, since we can
inhibit ovarian cancer cell invasion

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.360
and migration and block
both lymph node and

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.760
blood-borne metastasis
by blocking CD36.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.800
We see the same kind of
effect with prostate cancer.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.640
Suppress the uptake of fat
by prostate cancer cells,

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.440
and you can suppress the tumor.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.560
This was all studied with
receptor-blocking drugs and

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.320
antibodies in a laboratory
setting, though.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:17.080
If these metastasis-initiating
cancer cells particularly rely

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.920
on dietary fat to promote the
spread of cancer, why not just

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:24.160
block the dietary fat in the first place?

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:27.560
Cancer cells love fat and cholesterol.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.640
The reason why fat metabolism may
fuel cancer’s spread is because

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.840
there is so much energy stored in fat.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.040
“Hence, … metastatic cells might
take advantage of this feature

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.280
to obtain the high amount of energy
that is likely to be required

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.880
for them to anchor and [set up
shop throughout the body].”

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.600
“The time when sugar was considered
as the major, if not only,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.480
fuel to support cancer cell
proliferation is over.”

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.000
There appears to be” a
fatter way to metastasize”.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.480
No wonder “…high-fat [diets may] …play
a crucial role in increasing the risk

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:04.720
of different cancer types, …[including]
several advanced cancers.”

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.840
Okay, if dietary fat may be “greasing
the wheels of the cancer machine”

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.960
might there be speciﬁc dietary
regimens we could use

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.640
to starve cancers of dietary fat?

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:22.920
You don’t know until you put it
to the test, which we’ll cover next.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.320
“Metastasis is the leading cause
of cancer-[related death].”

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:31.360
Cancer kills, because cancer spreads.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.440
For example, the five-year
survival rate for women

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:36.600
with localized breast cancer
is nearly 99 percent,

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:42.160
but that drops to just 27 percent
in those with metastasized cancer.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.200
Yet “our ability to eﬀectively
treat metastatic disease

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.280
has not changed signiﬁcantly
in the past few decades”.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.760
You know things are getting desperate
when there are papers like this:

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.720
“Targeting Metastasis
with Snake Toxins . . .”

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:01.400
Now we do have built-in
defenses, natural killer cells

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.480
that roam the body killing
off budding tumors,

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.640
and I have videos on boosting
natural killer cell activity.

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:12.040
But as I explained in the last
video, there’s a fat receptor

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:16.160
called CD36 that appears to be
essential for cancer cells to spread,

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.960
and these cancer cells respond
to dietary fat intake.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.200
But not all fat.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:25.760
CD36 is upregulated
by palmitic acid,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.640
as much as a 50-fold
increase within 12 hours.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.440
Palmitic acid is a saturated
fat found in junk food

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:37.040
made from palm oil, but it is
most concentrated in meat and dairy.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:38.920
This may explain why if you look at

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.480
“dietary fat and breast cancer mortality”,

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:44.440
there was no difference in risk
of breast-cancer-speciﬁc death

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:48.760
for women in the highest versus
lowest category of total fat intake,

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:51.520
but you’re about 50 percent more
likely to die of breast cancer

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:54.760
if you eat a lot of saturated fat.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.440
The systematic review and
“…meta-analyses [conclude]… that

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.920
saturated fat intake negatively
impacts upon breast cancer survival.”

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.440
This may explain why
“intake of high-fat dairy,

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.000
but not low-fat dairy,
was related to a higher risk

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.840
of mortality after
breast cancer diagnosis.”

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:15.320
If it was the dairy protein,
like casein that was a problem,

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:17.600
the skim milk might be even worse;

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:21.240
but no, it was the saturated
butterfat, maybe because it triggered

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.200
that CD36-induced cancer
spreading mechanism.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.520
“…Women who consumed one
or more servings per day

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.760
of high-fat dairy had …
[about a 50] percent higher risk

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.920
of dying from breast cancer.”

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:36.360
We see the same thing with
“dairy intake in relation

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:39.640
to prostate cancer survival”.
“…Drinking high-fat milk

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.480
[appeared to increase] the risk
of dying from prostate cancer

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:48.000
by as much as 600 percent in patients
with localized prostate cancer”,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.600
but “low-fat milk was not associated
with such an increase in risk.”

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:55.720
So, it seemed to be the animal fat,
rather than the animal protein,

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:58.280
and this is consistent with
what Harvard researchers

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.000
found in the United States.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.640
More evidence that the fat
receptor CD36 is involved is

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.680
“…that the risk of colorectal
cancer for meat consumption

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.280
increased from [just doubling
risk to octupling risk]”—

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.880
multiplying the odds of
getting cancer eight-fold

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:19.000
for those who carry a
specific type of CD36 gene.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.360
So, “Is It Time to Give
Breast Cancer Patients

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:25.480
a Prescription for a Low-Fat Diet?”

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:30.240
A cancer diagnosis is a “teachable
moment” if there ever was one

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:33.280
to motivate people to make
changes to their lifestyle,

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:37.400
but provision of evidence-based
guidelines is essential

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:41.920
and you don’t know...
until you put it to the test.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.840
“A randomized, prospective,
multicenter clinical trial

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:48.000
to test the effect of a
dietary intervention designed

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.840
to reduce fat intake in
women with resected,

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.560
early-stage breast cancer…"
(meaning the women had their

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.560
breast cancer surgically removed and
were praying it doesn’t come back.)

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:02.000
The dietary intervention group
dropped their fat intake

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.680
from about 30 percent of calories
down to 20 percent of calories,

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:09.600
dropped saturated fat
intake about 40 percent

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.400
and maintained that 40 percent
lower intake after one year,

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:18.600
three years, five years. And…
“after approximately five years

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.120
of follow-up, [the] women in
the dietary intervention group

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:24.120
had a 24 percent lower risk of relapse…”,

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:29.440
a 24-percent lower risk of
the cancer coming back.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:33.880
That was the WINS study, the Women’s
Intervention Nutrition Study.

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:37.240
Then there was the Women’s Health
Initiative study, where again,

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:39.680
women were randomized to
drop their fat intake down

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.280
to about 20 percent of
calories, and again,

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:45.080
those in the dietary intervention
group experienced increased

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:48.560
breast cancer survival, meaning
“a dietary change may be able

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:50.960
to influence breast cancer outcome[s].”

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:55.040
And not only was breast cancer
survival significantly greater;

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:57.840
the women also experienced a
reduction in heart disease

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.261
and a reduction in diabetes
as a little side bonus.

